{"id":"s1.3.1","slug":"extending-foiling","title":"Extending Foiling","content":"<p class=\"mb-4\">The FOILing method can be extended to multiply two three-digit numbers by treating the last two digits of each number as a single unit. This effectively turns a three-digit multiplication into a more manageable two-digit multiplication problem.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">For example, to multiply $abc \\times def$, you can group them as $[a | bc] \\times [d | ef]$. The steps are:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4\"><li><strong>Last:</strong> Multiply the two-digit units: $bc \\times ef$. The last two digits of this product are the last two digits of your final answer. Keep track of the carry.</li><li><strong>Inner+Outer:</strong> Multiply the inner and outer pairs and add them, plus the carry: $(a \\times ef) + (d \\times bc) + carry$. The last two digits of this result are the next two digits of your answer. Again, track the new carry.</li><li><strong>First:</strong> Multiply the leading digits and add the final carry: $a \\times d + carry$.</li></ul><p>This method is particularly effective for squaring three-digit numbers, where memorizing squares of two-digit numbers is highly beneficial.</p>","summary":"The FOILing method can be extended to multiply two three-digit numbers by treating the last two digits of each number as a single unit.","generation_guideline":"Generate a problem involving the multiplication of two 3-digit numbers.","questions":[{"id":1,"text":"Calculate $202^2$.","answer":"40804","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":2,"text":"Calculate $406 \\times 406$.","answer":"164836","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":3,"text":"Calculate $503 \\times 503$.","answer":"253009","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":4,"text":"Calculate $607^2$.","answer":"368449","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":5,"text":"Calculate $208^2$.","answer":"43264","kind":"exact","tags":["arithmetic"]},{"id":6,"text":"Calculate $306^2$.","answer":"9